            <ArticleTitle>Maternal parenting behaviors during childhood relate to weight status and fruit and vegetable intake of college students.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>556-63</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jneb.2011.05.008</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1499-4046(11)00381-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Examine how maternal parenting behaviors in childhood, both general and feeding specific, relate to weight status and fruit and vegetable consumption in college students.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective surveys on maternal behaviors and assessments on the college-aged child's current anthropometric measures and dietary intakes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">College students (n = 424; 66% women).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Students' weight, height, waist circumference, fruit and vegetable intakes, students' reports on mothers' general and feeding-specific parenting behaviors during childhood.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="ANALYSIS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Correlation and regression analyses tested how maternal behaviors in childhood related to students' body mass index, waist circumference, and fruit and vegetable intake.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Mothers' psychological control during childhood was associated with higher body mass index and waist circumference in students, and behavioral control was associated with lower waist circumference. Parent-centered feeding behaviors related to lower fruit and vegetable intakes of students, whereas child-centered feeding behaviors related to higher fruit and vegetable intakes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS AND IMPLICATIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Findings suggest that parental use of behavioral control and child-centered feeding practices and minimal use of psychological control and parent-centered feeding practices during childhood may promote a child's healthful weight status and fruit and vegetable consumption in young adulthood, specifically during college.</AbstractText>
